  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\nA brief period of mid-level ridging will provide dry conditions\ntoday, though a weak disturbance will bring an increase in high and\nmid level clouds through the day.\n\nOn Sunday, a strong mid-level shortwave will move east from the\nmiddle Mississippi Valley region. At the surface, a cold front with\nan embedded surface low will cross the Ohio Valley, with the low\nexpected to move through the area some time late Sunday evening.\nStrong southerly flow will advect theta-e into the area ahead of the\nfront and surface low, followed by an abrupt shift to northwest flow\nafter the low has passed the area. With reasonably strong forcing,\nthough this system will be fairly progressive, rainfall amounts of a\nsolid half inch are expected area-wide, with some areas receiving up\nto an inch or even possibly more in the northwestern sections of the\nILN CWA. The axis of heaviest rainfall will likely be displaced just\nnorthwest of the surface low track, across the northern two-thirds of\nIndiana and into southern Michigan. Some very weak elevated\ninstability may also advect into the region, so thunder has been\nincluded in the forecast. The wind fields are strong enough that if\ninstability forecasts increase, some low-end severe threat could\nreach into the ILN Kentucky counties. For now, it seems like this\nthreat may remain just south of the area.\n\nThere will also be some gusty winds associated with this system. Some\n25-35 MPH gusts could occur in the warm advection, mainly near and\nsouth of the surface low track. A greater chance for gusty winds\nwill occur behind the cold front and surface low, with some 30-40 MPH\ngusts as the cold advection kicks in.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\n\nA large area of surface high pressure will bring dry conditions to\nthe Ohio Valley for the first several days of the week. There will\nalso be a gradual warming trend, with temperatures getting above\nnormal by Wednesday, and possibly well above normal for Thursday and\nFriday. Confidence is low in any details for the next chance of\nprecipitation, but it looks like the synoptic pattern may support\nanother weather system moving into the area by Friday.",
